  ("and many others which will be found in good dictionaries." 0.0920 0.5913 0.4844 0.5009 0.5009 0.0165 0.0048 0.3507 0.0326)
  ("(1) One can make an animal’s sex clear by adding macho ‘male’ or hembra ‘female’: la ardilla macho" 0.0920 0.9080 0.5131 0.5296 0.5293 0.0165 0.0041 0.3934 0.0225)
  ("‘male squirrel’, el cangrejo hembra ‘female crab’. In good Spanish, an adjective agrees with the" 0.0919 0.9080 0.5296 0.5461 0.5460 0.0165 0.0071 0.3017 0.0468)
  ("gender of the noun not of the animal itself: la rana macho está muerta ‘the male frog is dead’, un" 0.0919 0.9080 0.5461 0.5625 0.5625 0.0165 0.0057 0.3228 0.0597)
  ("cisne hembra blanco ‘a white female swan’. Macho and hembra are invariable: las cebras macho ‘male" 0.0919 0.9081 0.5626 0.5790 0.5790 0.0165 0.0045 0.3778 0.0389)
  ("zebras’, los gavilanes hembra ‘female sparrowhawks." 0.0920 0.5262 0.5791 0.5955 0.5955 0.0165 0.0048 0.3406 0.0651)
  ("Familiar language may say things like el/la gorila ‘he-gorilla’ and ‘she-gorilla’ (properly invariably" 0.0920 0.9081 0.6078 0.6242 0.6241 0.0165 0.0037 0.3585 0.0714)
  ("el gorila)." 0.0920 0.1658 0.6244 0.6406 0.6406 0.0163 0.0048 0.2308 0.0128)
  ("(2) La canguro (‘she-kangaroo’) is used in Spain for a female child-minder or baby-sitter." 0.0920 0.8380 0.6529 0.6694 0.6692 0.0165 0.0048 0.3170 0.0225)
  ("1.3.2 Gender of nouns referring to non-living things, to plants" 0.0920 0.8085 0.6934 0.7142 0.7142 0.0208 0.0068 0.3295 0.1515)
  ("and to other animals" 0.1582 0.3989 0.7135 0.7343 0.7343 0.0208 0.0068 0.3493 0.0433)
  ("The gender of nouns referring to non-living things, to plants and to the animals mentioned" 0.0920 0.9081 0.7420 0.7584 0.7584 0.0165 0.0080 0.2611 0.0322)
  ("in 1.3.1 must be learned for each noun. It has no sexual implications and it sometimes varies" 0.0920 0.9081 0.7585 0.7749 0.7749 0.0165 0.0070 0.3042 0.0168)
  ("from place to place: cf. sauna ‘sauna’, feminine in Spain, either gender in Latin America; sartén" 0.0920 0.9080 0.7749 0.7914 0.7913 0.0165 0.0061 0.3268 0.0412)
  ("‘frying pan’/US ‘skillet’, feminine in Spain, often masculine in Latin America. The gender of" 0.0920 0.9081 0.7914 0.8079 0.8079 0.0165 0.0073 0.3216 0.0576)
  ("some nouns also occasionally changes with time: cf. seventeenth-century la puente, now el puente" 0.0920 0.9080 0.8079 0.8244 0.8243 0.0165 0.0047 0.3362 0.0449)
  ("‘bridge’ (occasionally still la puente in some regions). El maratón and la maratón ‘marathon’ are" 0.0920 0.9080 0.8244 0.8409 0.8408 0.0165 0.0065 0.3039 0.0663)
  ("both current nowadays: El País insists on el maratón." 0.0920 0.5315 0.8409 0.8574 0.8573 0.0165 0.0048 0.3317 0.0387)
  ("There are few infallible rules and we quote only those which in our view do not encourage false" 0.0920 0.9079 0.8696 0.8861 0.8861 0.0165 0.0049 0.3361 0.0487)
  ("generalizations." 0.0920 0.2266 0.8861 0.9026 0.9026 0.0165 nil 0.2293 0.1347))
  (21
  ("8 Gender of nouns" 0.0905 0.2404 0.0492 0.0683 0.0679 0.0154 0.0046 0.3029 0.0124)
  ("1.3.3 Masculine by meaning" 0.0920 0.4191 0.0825 0.1033 0.1033 0.0208 0.0068 0.3530 0.0541)
  ("Some of these have acquired the gender of an underlying omitted noun:" 0.0920 0.7038 0.1110 0.1275 0.1275 0.0165 0.0048 0.3164 0.0469)
  ("(a) Rivers (el río): el Amazonas ‘the Amazon’, el Jarama, el Manzanares, el Sena ‘the Seine’, el Támesis" 0.0920 0.9080 0.1397 0.1562 0.1561 0.0165 0.0043 0.3888 0.0225)
  ("‘the Thames’, el Volga. Locally some rivers may be feminine, but outsiders rarely know this and" 0.0919 0.9080 0.1562 0.1727 0.1726 0.0165 0.0054 0.3519 0.0321)
  ("the masculine is always correct." 0.0919 0.3609 0.1727 0.1892 0.1892 0.0165 0.0048 0.3455 0.0267)
  ("(b) Mountains, oceans, seas and lakes (el monte, el océano, el mar, el lago): los Alpes, el Etna, el" 0.0919 0.9080 0.2014 0.2179 0.2178 0.0165 0.0075 0.2945 0.0246)
  ("Everest, el Himalaya (singular), el Pacífico, el Caribe ‘Caribbean’, el Windermere." 0.0919 0.7368 0.2179 0.2344 0.2343 0.0165 0.0048 0.3666 0.0623)
  ("(c) The names of cars, boats and aircraft (el coche, el barco, el avión): un Toyota, un Mercedes, el caza" 0.0919 0.9080 0.2466 0.2631 0.2630 0.0165 0.0050 0.3174 0.0214)
  ("‘fighter plane’, el Queen Elizabeth, el Marie Celeste, un DC10, un Mig-31. But small boats (la barca)" 0.0920 0.9080 0.2631 0.2796 0.2795 0.0165 0.0056 0.3636 0.0620)
  ("are usually feminine, as are light aircraft because of the noun la avioneta: una Cessna." 0.0920 0.8017 0.2796 0.2961 0.2960 0.0165 0.0048 0.3115 0.0261)
  ("(d) Months and days of the week (los meses y los días de la semana): enero/abril pasado, el lunes" 0.0920 0.9081 0.3083 0.3247 0.3246 0.0165 0.0074 0.2796 0.0246)
  ("‘Monday’, un viernes frío ‘a cold Friday’, etc." 0.0920 0.4636 0.3248 0.3412 0.3411 0.0165 0.0048 0.3620 0.0876)
  ("(e) Wines (el vino): el Borgoña ‘Burgundy’, el Chianti, un Rioja, el champaña ‘champagne’, usually el" 0.0920 0.9081 0.3534 0.3699 0.3698 0.0165 0.0044 0.3673 0.0225)
  ("champán in spoken Spanish, but la champaña in Mexico, Colombia and Venezuela. El cava is used" 0.0920 0.9081 0.3699 0.3864 0.3863 0.0165 0.0051 0.3304 0.0699)
  ("to refer to champagne made in Spain." 0.0920 0.4103 0.3864 0.4029 0.4029 0.0165 0.0048 0.3448 0.0168)
  ("(f) Pictures (el cuadro) by named artists: un Constable, un Leonardo, un Rembrandt, un Riley." 0.0920 0.8484 0.4151 0.4316 0.4315 0.0165 0.0048 0.3188 0.0203)
  ("(g) Sports teams (el equipo): el Barça ‘Barcelona FC’ (pronounced [báɾ-sa]), el Betis (one of Seville’s" 0.0920 0.9081 0.4438 0.4603 0.4601 0.0165 0.0044 0.3277 0.0235)
  ("soccer teams), el Real Madrid, etc." 0.0920 0.3698 0.4603 0.4768 0.4767 0.0165 0.0048 0.3879 0.0526)
  ("(h) All infinitives, and all quoted words: el fumar ‘smoking’, el escupir ‘spitting’, ‘“mujer” es feme-" 0.0920 0.9082 0.4890 0.5055 0.5054 0.0165 0.0048 0.3780 0.0246)
  ("nino ‘(the word) ‘“mujer” is feminine’, no viene la señal, el “siga” que él esperaba (EP, Mex.) ‘the" 0.0920 0.9081 0.5055 0.5220 0.5219 0.0165 0.0070 0.3139 0.0375)
  ("signal doesn’t come, the “go on” that he was expecting’." 0.0920 0.5680 0.5220 0.5385 0.5385 0.0165 0.0048 0.3335 0.0509)
  ("(i) Any adverb, interjection or other genderless word used as a noun: el más allá ‘the Beyond’," 0.0920 0.9081 0.5507 0.5672 0.5671 0.0165 0.0063 0.2876 0.0192)
  ("un algo ‘a “something”’, tiene un no sabe uno qué que gusta (LRS, Puerto Rico, dialogue) ‘she’s got" 0.0920 0.9081 0.5672 0.5837 0.5836 0.0165 0.0053 0.3061 0.0214)
  ("something or other pleasing about her’." 0.0920 0.4290 0.5837 0.6002 0.6002 0.0165 0.0048 0.3111 0.0899)
  ("(j) Numbers (el número): un seis, un 5, la Generación del 98 the ‘Generation of ’98’, el dos por ciento" 0.0920 0.9080 0.6124 0.6289 0.6288 0.0165 0.0052 0.3362 0.0192)
  ("‘two per cent’." 0.0920 0.2136 0.6289 0.6454 0.6454 0.0165 0.0048 0.3739 0.0382)
  ("(k) Musical notes: el fa, el la (underlying noun unclear)." 0.0920 0.5615 0.6576 0.6741 0.6740 0.0165 0.0048 0.3479 0.0246)
  ("(l) Colours (el color): el azul ‘blue’, el ocre ‘ochre’; se amplía el naranja del horizonte ‘the orange of the" 0.0920 0.9080 0.6863 0.7028 0.7027 0.0165 0.0042 0.3284 0.0192)
  ("horizon is spreading’ (AG, Sp.), mandaron instalar una alfombra verde aunque no hiciera juego con el" 0.0920 0.9080 0.7028 0.7193 0.7192 0.0165 0.0053 0.3051 0.0663)
  ("rosa pálido de las paredes (ES, Mex.) ‘they had a green carpet laid even though it didn’t match the" 0.0920 0.9080 0.7193 0.7358 0.7357 0.0165 0.0053 0.3223 0.0317)
  ("pale pink of the walls’." 0.0920 0.2852 0.7358 0.7523 0.7523 0.0165 0.0048 0.3673 0.0360)
