  ("and most other scientific or technical words ending in -ma. But la amalgama ‘amalgam’, el asma" 0.0920 0.9079 0.6932 0.7096 0.7095 0.0165 0.0062 0.3484 0.0326)
  ("‘asthma’ (feminine, see 3.1.2 for the el), la estratagema ‘stratagem’ and la flema ‘phlegm’ are femi-" 0.0920 0.9080 0.7097 0.7261 0.7260 0.0165 0.0053 0.3784 0.0726)
  ("nine. For other feminine words ending in -ma see 1.3.8." 0.0920 0.5568 0.7261 0.7426 0.7425 0.0165 0.0048 0.3583 0.0421)
  ("(1) El Sáhara ‘the Sahara’, pronounced as though written sájara, has more or less replaced the" 0.0920 0.9081 0.7548 0.7713 0.7711 0.0165 0.0068 0.2634 0.0225)
  ("older form el Sahara (pronounced [sa-á-ra]). El País rejects the latter form." 0.0919 0.7101 0.7713 0.7878 0.7877 0.0165 0.0048 0.3302 0.0447)
  ("(2) ‘Pyjamas’/US ‘pajamas’ is la pajama or la piyama in Mexico, the Caribbean and much of" 0.0919 0.9081 0.8000 0.8165 0.8163 0.0165 0.0083 0.3132 0.0225)
  ("Central America: en piyama te ves soñada (EM, Mex., dialogue; Spain pareces un sueño en pijama)" 0.0919 0.9081 0.8165 0.8330 0.8329 0.0165 0.0063 0.3335 0.0632)
  ("‘you look a dream in pyjamas’." 0.0919 0.3543 0.8330 0.8495 0.8495 0.0165 0.0048 0.3811 0.0382)
  ("(3) A few masculine words ending in -ma are made feminine in popular speech, dialects and" 0.0919 0.9081 0.8617 0.8782 0.8780 0.0165 0.0070 0.3147 0.0225)
  ("pre-nineteenth-century texts, especially clima, miasma and fantasma, cf. pobre fantasma soñadora in" 0.0919 0.9080 0.8782 0.8947 0.8946 0.0165 0.0052 0.3119 0.1968)
  ("Lorca’s El maleficio de la mariposa." 0.0919 0.3691 0.8947 0.9112 0.9111 0.0165 0.0048 0.3448 0.0613))
